.Sh DESCRIPTION
This documentation is primarily aimed at provider authors.
See
.Xr provider 7
for further information.
.Pp
The asymmetric kem (OSSL_OP_KEM) operation enables providers to
implement asymmetric kem algorithms and make them available to
applications via the API functions
.Xr EVP_PKEY_encapsulate 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_decapsulate 3
and other related functions.
.Pp
All "functions" mentioned here are passed as function pointers between
.Pa libcrypto
and the provider in
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays via
.Xr OSSL_ALGORITHM 3
arrays that are returned by the provider's
.Xr provider_query_operation 3
function (see
.Xr provider-base 7 Ns ).
.Pp
All these "functions" have a corresponding function type definition
named
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_{name}_fn ,
and a helper function to retrieve the function pointer from an
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
element named
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_{name} .
For example, the "function"
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_newctx
has these:
.Bd -literal
 typedef void *(OSSL_FUNC_kem_newctx_fn)(void *provctx);
 static ossl_inline OSSL_FUNC_kem_newctx_fn
     OSSL_FUNC_kem_newctx(const OSSL_DISPATCH *opf);
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays are indexed by numbers that are provided as macros in
.Xr openssl-core_dispatch.h 7 ,
as follows:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_newctx                OSSL_FUNC_KEM_NEWCTX
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_freectx               OSSL_FUNC_KEM_FREECTX
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_dupctx                OSSL_FUNC_KEM_DUPCTX

 OSSL_FUNC_kem_encapsulate_init      OSSL_FUNC_KEM_ENCAPSULATE_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_auth_encapsulate_init OSSL_FUNC_KEM_AUTH_ENCAPSULATE_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_encapsulate           OSSL_FUNC_KEM_ENCAPSULATE

 OSSL_FUNC_kem_decapsulate_init      OSSL_FUNC_KEM_DECAPSULATE_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_auth_decapsulate_init OSSL_FUNC_KEM_AUTH_DECAPSULATE_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_decapsulate           OSSL_FUNC_KEM_DECAPSULATE

 OSSL_FUNC_kem_get_ctx_params        OSSL_FUNC_KEM_GET_CTX_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_gettable_ctx_params   OSSL_FUNC_KEM_GETTABLE_CTX_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_set_ctx_params        OSSL_FUNC_KEM_SET_CTX_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_kem_settable_ctx_params   OSSL_FUNC_KEM_SETTABLE_CTX_PARAMS
.Ed
.Pp
An asymmetric kem algorithm implementation may not implement all of
these functions.
In order to be a consistent set of functions a provider must implement
OSSL_FUNC_kem_newctx and OSSL_FUNC_kem_freectx.
It must also implement both of OSSL_FUNC_kem_encapsulate_init and
OSSL_FUNC_kem_encapsulate, or both of OSSL_FUNC_kem_decapsulate_init and
OSSL_FUNC_kem_decapsulate.
OSSL_FUNC_kem_auth_encapsulate_init is optional but if it is present
then so must OSSL_FUNC_kem_auth_decapsulate_init.
OSSL_FUNC_kem_get_ctx_params is optional but if it is present then so
must OSSL_FUNC_kem_gettable_ctx_params.
Similarly, OSSL_FUNC_kem_set_ctx_params is optional but if it is present
then OSSL_FUNC_kem_settable_ctx_params must also be present.
.Pp
An asymmetric kem algorithm must also implement some mechanism for
generating, loading or importing keys via the key management
(OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation.
See
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7
for further details.
.Ss Context Management Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_newctx
should create and return a pointer to a provider side structure for
holding context information during an asymmetric kem operation.
A pointer to this context will be passed back in a number of the other
asymmetric kem operation function calls.
The parameter
.Fa provctx
is the provider context generated during provider initialisation (see
.Xr provider 7 Ns ).
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_freectx
is passed a pointer to the provider side asymmetric kem context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
This function should free any resources associated with that context.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_dupctx
should duplicate the provider side asymmetric kem context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter and return the duplicate copy.
.Ss Asymmetric Key Encapsulation Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_encapsulate_init
initialises a context for an asymmetric encapsulation given a provider
side asymmetric kem context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter, a pointer to a provider key object in the
.Fa provkey
parameter and the
.Em name
of the algorithm.
The
.Em params ,
if not NULL, should be set on the context in a manner similar to using
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_set_ctx_params .
The key object should have been previously generated, loaded or imported
into the provider using the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation
(see provider-keymgmt(7)>.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_auth_encapsulate_init
is similar to
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_encapsulate_init ,
but also passes an additional authentication key
.Fa provauthkey
which cannot be NULL.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_encapsulate
performs the actual encapsulation itself.
A previously initialised asymmetric kem context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
Unless
.Fa out
is NULL, the data to be encapsulated is internally generated, and
returned into the buffer pointed to by the
.Fa secret
parameter and the encapsulated data should also be written to the
location pointed to by the
.Fa out
parameter.
The length of the encapsulated data should be written to
.Em *outlen
and the length of the generated secret should be written to
.Em *secretlen .
.Pp
If
.Fa out
is NULL then the maximum length of the encapsulated data should be
written to
.Em *outlen ,
and the maximum length of the generated secret should be written to
.Em *secretlen .
.Ss Decapsulation Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_decapsulate_init
initialises a context for an asymmetric decapsulation given a provider
side asymmetric kem context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter, a pointer to a provider key object in the
.Fa provkey
parameter, and a
.Em name
of the algorithm.
The key object should have been previously generated, loaded or imported
into the provider using the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation
(see provider-keymgmt(7)>.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_auth_decapsulate_init
is similar to
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_decapsulate_init ,
but also passes an additional authentication key
.Fa provauthkey
which cannot be NULL.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_decapsulate
performs the actual decapsulation itself.
A previously initialised asymmetric kem context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
The data to be decapsulated is pointed to by the
.Fa in
parameter which is
.Fa inlen
bytes long.
Unless
.Fa out
is NULL, the decapsulated data should be written to the location pointed
to by the
.Fa out
parameter.
The length of the decapsulated data should be written to
.Em *outlen .
If
.Fa out
is NULL then the maximum length of the decapsulated data should be
written to
.Em *outlen .
.Ss Asymmetric Key Encapsulation Parameters
See
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
for further details on the parameters structure used by the
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_get_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_set_ctx_params
functions.
.Pp
The OpenSSL FIPS provider also supports the following parameters:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqfips-indicator" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KEM_PARAM_FIPS_APPROVED_INDICATOR Ns ) <integer>
A getter that returns 1 if the operation is FIPS approved, or 0
otherwise.
This may be used after calling either
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_encapsulate
or
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_decapsulate .
It may return 0 if the "key-check" is set to 0.
.It \(dqkey-check" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KEM_PARAM_FIPS_KEY_CHECK Ns ) <integer>
If required this parameter should be set using
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_encapsulate_init
or
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_decapsulate_init .
The default value of 1 causes an error during the init if the key is not
FIPS approved (e.g.
The key has a security strength of less than 112 bits).
Setting this to 0 will ignore the error and set the approved
"fips-indicator" to 0. This option breaks FIPS compliance if it causes
the approved "fips-indicator" to return 0.
.El
.Ss Asymmetric Key Encapsulation Parameter Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_get_ctx_params
gets asymmetric KEM parameters associated with the given provider side
asymmetric kem context
.Fa ctx
and stores them in
.Em params .
Passing NULL for
.Em params
should return true.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_set_ctx_params
sets the asymmetric KEM parameters associated with the given provider
side asymmetric kem context
.Fa ctx
to
.Em params .
Any parameter settings are additional to any that were previously set.
Passing NULL for
.Em params
should return true.
.Pp
No parameters are currently recognised by built-in asymmetric kem
algorithms.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_gettable_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_settable_ctx_params
get a constant
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
array that describes the gettable and settable parameters, i.e.
parameters that can be used with
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_get_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_set_ctx_params
respectively.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_newctx
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_dupctx
should return the newly created provider side asymmetric kem context, or
NULL on failure.
.Pp
All other functions should return 1 for success or 0 on error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr provider 7
.Sh HISTORY
The provider KEM interface was introduced in OpenSSL 3.0.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_auth_encapsulate_init
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_kem_auth_decapsulate_init
were added in OpenSSL 3.2.
.Pp
The Asymmetric Key Encapsulation Parameters "fips-indicator" and
"key-check" were added in OpenSSL 3.4.
